Derive the breadcrumb from TanStack Router’s [active route matches](https://tanstack.com/router/latest/docs/api/router/useMatchesHook). Define each title once with [`staticData`](https://tanstack.com/router/latest/docs/guide/static-route-data) on the route. The shared layout builds the trail from the matched route hierarchy, including the resolved path parameters.

```tsx
// routes/projects.tsx
import { createFileRoute, Outlet } from "@tanstack/react-router";

export const Route = createFileRoute("/projects")({
  component: Outlet,
  staticData: { breadcrumb: "Projects" },
});
```

Set `staticData: { breadcrumb: "Settings" }` on the nested settings route. Render `RouteBreadcrumb` in the shared layout alongside its `Outlet`:

```tsx
import { trimPathRight, useMatches } from "@tanstack/react-router";
import { Breadcrumb } from "@erbium/ui/components/breadcrumb";
import { Link } from "@erbium/ui/components/link";

declare module "@tanstack/react-router" {
  interface StaticDataRouteOption {
    breadcrumb?: string;
  }
}

export const RouteBreadcrumb = () => {
  const matches = useMatches();
  const labels = new Map<string, string>();

  for (const match of matches) {
    const label = match.staticData.breadcrumb;
    if (label !== undefined) {
      labels.set(trimPathRight(match.pathname), label);
    }
  }

  const leaf = matches.at(-1);
  if (!leaf) {
    return null;
  }

  const currentPath = trimPathRight(leaf.pathname);
  const currentPage = labels.get(currentPath);
  if (currentPage === undefined) {
    return null;
  }

  labels.delete(currentPath);
  return (
    <Breadcrumb
      items={[...labels].map(([to, label]) => ({
        id: to,
        label,
        render: <Link to={to} activeOptions={{ exact: true }} />,
      }))}
      currentPage={currentPage}
    />
  );
};
```

Leave metadata off routes that should not add a crumb, such as pathless layouts. If a layout and its index share a URL, the deeper labeled match wins. The example skips pages with no label for the current URL. For a title that depends on fetched data, such as a project name, read that route’s loader data in the same adapter. If an ancestor requires search parameters, pass the search values required by that route to its link.

The preview uses in-memory routing. Select **Projects** to go up one level and **Open settings** to return. The same links work in the overflow menu when the trail collapses.

You can turn a bubble into a link or button by using the `render` prop on `BubbleContent`.

```tsx
import { Bubble, BubbleContent } from "@erbium/ui/components/bubble";

export function BubbleLinkDemo() {
  return (
    <Bubble variant="muted">
      <BubbleContent render={<button />}>Click here</BubbleContent>
    </Bubble>
  );
}
```

You can use the `buttonVariants` helper to make a link look like a button.

**Do not use `<Button render={<a />} nativeButton={false} />` for links.** The Base UI `Button` component always applies `role="button"`, which overrides the semantic link role on `<a>` elements. Use `buttonVariants` with a plain `<a>` tag instead.

For a TanStack Router destination, apply the same styles to Erbium's `Link` to enable Foresight route preloading.

```tsx
import { Link } from "@erbium/ui/components/link";
import { buttonVariants } from "@erbium/ui/lib/button-variants";

<Link to="/projects" className={buttonVariants()}>
  Projects
</Link>;
```

Pass `value` and `onValueChange` to control the position from your own state. Any control can then move the carousel, and the carousel reports swipes and key presses back through `onValueChange`. `CarouselIndicator` and `CarouselCounter` follow the position without extra wiring. A new `value` animates only while the carousel is on screen and uncovered; otherwise the carousel jumps straight to it.

```tsx
const [value, setValue] = React.useState(0);

<Carousel aria-label="Workflow" value={value} onValueChange={setValue}>
  <CarouselSlides>...</CarouselSlides>
  <CarouselIndicator />
  <CarouselCounter />
</Carousel>;
```

To build a control inside the carousel, read `useCarousel()`:

```tsx
const JumpToEnd = () => {
  const { state, actions } = useCarousel();

  return <Button onClick={() => actions.goTo(state.count - 1)}>Last</Button>;
};
```

`onValueChange` receives the new position and a `source` that says what moved the carousel: `button`, `drag`, `focus`, `indicator`, `keyboard`, `autoplay`, or `imperative`. Use it for analytics, or to react only to the person's own navigation.

```tsx
<Carousel
  aria-label="Numbers"
  onValueChange={(value, { source }) => {
    console.log(`Slide ${value + 1} via ${source}`);
  }}
>
  <CarouselSlides>...</CarouselSlides>
</Carousel>
```

A custom definition rendered with `Chart`: monthly revenue bars with a dashed target line. `chartTheme` and `chartTooltip` give it the same colors and tooltip as the chart types, and `ChartLegend` explains both marks.

```tsx
import {
  Chart,
  ChartLegend,
  chartTheme,
  chartTooltip,
  resolveChartSeries,
} from "@erbium/ui/charts/chart";
import { barY, defineChart, ruleY } from "@tanstack/charts";

const definition = defineChart({
  marks: [barY(revenue, { x: "month", y: "revenue", fill: "var(--chart-1)" }), ruleY([target])],
  scales,
  theme: chartTheme,
  tooltip: chartTooltip,
});
```

Use the `open` and `onOpenChange` props to control the state.

```tsx
import * as React from "react";

export function Example() {
  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);

  return (
    <Collapsible open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
      <CollapsibleTrigger>Toggle</CollapsibleTrigger>
      <CollapsibleContent>Content</CollapsibleContent>
    </Collapsible>
  );
}
```

Use `itemToStringValue` when your items are objects.

```tsx
import * as React from "react";

import {
  Combobox,
  ComboboxContent,
  ComboboxEmpty,
  ComboboxInput,
  ComboboxItem,
  ComboboxList,
} from "@erbium/ui/components/combobox";

type Framework = {
  label: string;
  value: string;
};

const frameworks: Framework[] = [
  { label: "TanStack Start", value: "tanstack-start" },
  { label: "SvelteKit", value: "sveltekit" },
  { label: "Nuxt", value: "nuxt" },
];

export function ExampleComboboxCustomItems() {
  return (
    <Combobox items={frameworks} itemToStringValue={(framework) => framework.label}>
      <ComboboxInput placeholder="Select a framework" />
      <ComboboxContent>
        <ComboboxEmpty>No items found.</ComboboxEmpty>
        <ComboboxList>
          {(framework) => (
            <ComboboxItem key={framework.value} value={framework}>
              {framework.label}
            </ComboboxItem>
          )}
        </ComboboxList>
      </ComboboxContent>
    </Combobox>
  );
}
```

You can render a custom component inside `ComboboxItem`.

Use `multiple` with chips for multi-select behavior.

```tsx
import * as React from "react";

import {
  Combobox,
  ComboboxChip,
  ComboboxChips,
  ComboboxChipsInput,
  ComboboxContent,
  ComboboxEmpty,
  ComboboxInput,
  ComboboxItem,
  ComboboxList,
  ComboboxValue,
} from "@erbium/ui/components/combobox";

const frameworks = ["TanStack Start", "SvelteKit", "Nuxt.js", "Remix", "Astro"];

export function ExampleComboboxMultiple() {
  const [value, setValue] = React.useState<string[]>([]);

  return (
    <Combobox items={frameworks} multiple value={value} onValueChange={setValue}>
      <ComboboxChips>
        <ComboboxValue>
          {value.map((item) => (
            <ComboboxChip key={item}>{item}</ComboboxChip>
          ))}
        </ComboboxValue>
        <ComboboxChipsInput placeholder="Add framework" />
      </ComboboxChips>
      <ComboboxContent>
        <ComboboxEmpty>No items found.</ComboboxEmpty>
        <ComboboxList>
          {(item) => (
            <ComboboxItem key={item} value={item}>
              {item}
            </ComboboxItem>
          )}
        </ComboboxList>
      </ComboboxContent>
    </Combobox>
  );
}
```

A combobox with multiple selection using `multiple` and `ComboboxChips`.

Turn a marker into a link or button with the `render` prop on `Marker`.

```tsx
import { Marker, MarkerContent } from "@erbium/ui/components/marker";

export function MarkerLinkDemo() {
  return (
    <Marker render={<a href="#" />}>
      <MarkerContent>View the pull request</MarkerContent>
    </Marker>
  );
}
```

In a TanStack Router app, pass Erbium's `Link` to `NavigationMenuLink` through `render`. The menu still manages focus and keyboard navigation, while Foresight can preload the destination before activation. The menu applies its active styles when the router marks a link with `aria-current="page"`. Use `href` for ordinary anchors.

```tsx
import { Link } from "@erbium/ui/components/link";

import { NavigationMenuItem, NavigationMenuLink } from "@erbium/ui/components/navigation-menu";

export function NavigationMenuDemo() {
  return (
    <NavigationMenuItem>
      <NavigationMenuLink render={<Link to="/docs" />}>Documentation</NavigationMenuLink>
    </NavigationMenuItem>
  );
}
```

Use the `open` and `onOpenChange` props to control the sidebar.

```tsx
export function AppSidebar() {
  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);

  return (
    <SidebarProvider open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
      <Sidebar />
    </SidebarProvider>
  );
}
```

`autoplay` fills the current pill over `duration` and then selects the next step, so readers can see how long is left before the content changes. It restarts whenever the value changes, whoever changed it. `paused` holds the fill where it is; tie it to a pause button, or to hover over the content it advances. `loop` (on by default) returns to the first step after the last. Reduced motion turns the timer off and shows a solid pill.

```tsx
const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);
const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);

<StepIndicator
  variant="dots"
  count={4}
  value={index}
  onValueChange={setIndex}
  autoplay={{ duration: 3000, paused }}
/>;
```

The clock moves the fill every frame without re-rendering. When something else owns the clock, pass a motion value to `progress` instead; `CarouselIndicator` does this with the carousel's autoplay timer.

Pass button props with `actionProps` to render an action. This example closes the toast; a real Undo handler must also restore application state.

```tsx
const id = toast.add({
  id: "event-created",
  title: "Event created",
  actionProps: {
    children: "Undo",
    onClick() {
      toast.close(id);
    },
  },
});
```
